# .dockerignore decides what goes into the build
# rsync decides what lands on the server.
# 
# Allowlist: send only what the Dockerfile COPYs, so secrets (.env), .git and
# local files never reach the build context or the image (e.g. on Docker Hub).
# 
# If the Dockerfile COPYs something new, add it here with "!".
# .dockerignore has the final veto on the Dockerfile:
#     - COPY of an excluded file fails ("not found")
#     - COPY of a folder silently skips excluded files
#     - the Dockerfile itself is always read, even if excluded
*
!app
!web
!environment.yml
!start.sh
!crontab
!server.py
!chromiumprofile

# ====================================
# Prune inside allowed folders
# ====================================
# Later rules win. Use "**/" to match at any depth (plain patterns match only the root).
# 
# If the app needs an excluded secret file (e.g. app/pysecrets.py):
#     1. Mount it at runtime in compose (volumes: ./app/pysecrets.py:/app/pysecrets.py:ro)
#     2. Move the values into .env and read them with os.getenv, so the file isn't needed at all

# Secrets
**/.env
**/.env.*
**/archive/
**/vault/
**/secret/
**/secrets/
**/secret.py
**/pysecrets.py
**/local_settings.py

# Local data
**/upload
**/instance
**/*.db
**/*.sqlite3
**/*.log

# Editors
**/.vscode
**/.idea

# OS
**/.DS_Store
**/._*
**/Thumbs.db

# Python
**/.venv
**/venv
**/env
**/__pycache__
**/*.py[cod]
**/.ipynb_checkpoints
**/.pytest_cache
**/.mypy_cache
**/.ruff_cache
**/.coverage
**/htmlcov

# Node
**/node_modules
